uvc: double the isochronous refill deadline

f_uvc raises a completion interrupt every

	min(DIV_ROUND_UP(video->uvc_num_requests, 4), UVCG_REQ_MAX_INT_COUNT)

requests (uvc_video.c:310), and uvc_video_complete() is what re-arms
uvcg_video_hw_submit() to queue the next batch.  UVCG_REQ_MAX_INT_COUNT
is 16 and does not depend on bInterval, so that cap is a wall-clock
deadline for the submit kthread that scales with the service interval:

	bInterval 1   16 x 125 us   =  2 ms
	bInterval 3   16 x 500 us   =  8 ms

Miss the deadline and dwc2 has nothing queued when the target frame
elapses, so it completes the next request -ENODATA, f_uvc raises
UVC_QUEUE_DROP_INCOMPLETE, and the frame ends where it stands.  On a
1 GHz single-core ARM11 sharing that core with the vchiq bulk transfers
carrying the encoded frames off VideoCore, that is the whole remaining
failure mode: not a systematic error but a latency tail.

Measured 2026-08-15 at bInterval 3, 720p30 H.264: 3 truncated frames in
690, ~6.5e-5 of requests.  The submit path already runs as SCHED_FIFO
(uvc_video.c:830) and the pump workqueue is already WQ_HIGHPRI, so there
is no priority left to claim - only a longer deadline.

Raising the cap to 32 doubles it:

	uvc_num_requests = nreq + UVCG_REQ_MAX_ZERO_COUNT = 67 + 64 = 131
	min(DIV_ROUND_UP(131, 4), 32) = min(33, 32) = 32
	32 x 500 us = 16 ms

and doubles the idle-window depth too, since UVCG_REQ_MAX_ZERO_COUNT is
defined as twice this: up to 65 zero-length requests queued ahead of the
hardware instead of 33, i.e. 32.5 ms rather than 16.5 ms of slack in the
gap between one frame draining and the next arriving.

The cost the comment above that condition names is "a trade-off between
latency and interrupt load", and both halves land the right way here.
Interrupt load is the scarce resource on this board, and the latency is
paid only mid-frame: the same condition short-circuits on ureq->last_buf,
so the final request of every buffer still raises an interrupt and buffer
turnaround to the daemon is unchanged.

Memory cost is 32 more requests of req_size (1024 on dwc2 high speed)
plus their scatterlists - about 33 KB.

Upstream picked 16 by admission rather than measurement.  From
dc97c956a470, which introduced the zero-length requests:

	To properly solve the amount of zero length requests that needs to
	be held in the hardware ... needs to be measured and depends on the
	runtime of the first enqueue run after the interrupt triggered. For
	now we just use twice the amount of requests between an interrupt.

This changes no request COUNT and no service interval, so it cannot
disturb dwc2's target-frame clock - the failure mode that killed both
/boot/uvc-overspeed above 1 and the pre-6.13 fixed request sizing.  It
only changes how often the driver is given a chance to keep that clock
wound.

NOT YET MEASURED.  3 events in 690 frames is a thin baseline to judge an
improvement against; shoot a long run before and after.

Signed-off-by: DashBerry <dashberry@localhost>

diff --git a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/uvc.h b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/uvc.h
--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/uvc.h
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/uvc.h
@@ -71,7 +71,15 @@

 #define UVCG_REQUEST_HEADER_LEN			12

-#define UVCG_REQ_MAX_INT_COUNT			16
+/*
+ * DashBerry: 32, not upstream's 16. This is the cap on how many requests may
+ * pass between completion interrupts, and since it does not scale with
+ * bInterval it is a wall-clock deadline for the submit kthread: 16 x 500 us =
+ * 8 ms at bInterval 3. Missing it costs a frame. 32 makes it 16 ms, and
+ * doubles the zero-length queue depth below with it. Paid only mid-frame -
+ * ureq->last_buf still forces an interrupt at the end of every buffer.
+ */
+#define UVCG_REQ_MAX_INT_COUNT			32
 #define UVCG_REQ_MAX_ZERO_COUNT			(2 * UVCG_REQ_MAX_INT_COUNT)

 #define UVCG_STREAMING_MIN_BUFFERS		2
